---
/**
 * The intake form. Posts to /api/contact.
 *
 * There used to be two of these, switched on a `kind` prop: this short one and
 * a long research questionnaire on /systems-map. The owner killed the systems
 * map offer on 14 Aug 2026 and that page is gone, so the prop and the second
 * branch went with it. One form, six fields.
 *
 * ⚠️ Field names here must match LIMITS and FIELDS in src/pages/api/contact.ts.
 * Adding a field in one place and not the other collects the answer and
 * silently drops it. See CLAUDE.md landmine 14.
 *
 * Native POST, no client bundle, works with JavaScript disabled.
 */
---

<form class="form" action="/api/contact" method="POST">
  {/* ⚠️ Honeypot, and note the name.

      It used to be `company_website`, labelled "Company website". The v3 copy
      asks visitors for their company website as a real question, so that name
      is now taken by a field real people fill in. Had both kept it, every
      genuine submission would have tripped the bot check in contact.ts, been
      dropped without an email, and still shown the visitor a success page.
      Silent lead loss with no error anywhere.

      The trap is the label as much as the name, so this one is a fax number:
      nothing on this site asks for one, and no visitor will ever see it.

      Positioned off-screen rather than display:none, which bots check for.
      Hidden from assistive tech and skipped by the tab order. */}
  <div class="hp" aria-hidden="true">
    <label for="f-contact-fax">Fax number</label>
    <input
      id="f-contact-fax"
      name="contact_fax"
      type="text"
      tabindex="-1"
      autocomplete="off"
    />
  </div>

  <label for="f-name">Name</label>
  <input id="f-name" name="name" type="text" autocomplete="name" required />

  <label for="f-email">Email</label>
  <input id="f-email" name="email" type="email" autocomplete="email" required />

  <label for="f-website">Company website</label>
  <input
    id="f-website"
    name="website"
    type="text"
    autocomplete="url"
    placeholder="threadhawaii.com"
  />

  {/* "your", not "the": every other question in the set says you. Label text
      only; the field name is the contract with contact.ts (landmine 14). */}
  <label for="f-biz">What does your business do?</label>
  <input id="f-biz" name="business" type="text" autocomplete="organization" />

  <label for="f-tools">What software are you running?</label>
  <textarea
    id="f-tools"
    name="tools"
    placeholder="A rough list is fine."
  ></textarea>

  <label for="f-timesink">What eats the most time right now?</label>
  <textarea
    id="f-timesink"
    name="timesink"
    placeholder="The thing that made you open this form."
  ></textarea>

  {/* "Show me your busywork" since 29 Aug 2026, from the design. It stays
      distinct from the site-wide CTA on purpose: everywhere else the label has
      to describe arriving at this form, and here it describes sending it. The
      one rule that has always applied to this button is that it must not say
      "Book a call", because pressing it sends a message and no calendar is
      involved. The arrow is a real glyph, not a pseudo-element, and resolves
      in JetBrains Mono — see the U+2192 alias in global.css. */}
  <button type="submit" class="btn">Show me your busywork <span class="arw" aria-hidden="true">&#8594;</span></button>

  {/* One reply-time string across the whole site since 27 Aug 2026. It existed
      in three variants: this note said "usually same day", the §04 lead said
      "within a business day, usually sooner", and /thanks said "within one
      business day, usually the same day". Three promises for one behaviour. If
      this changes, change it in ContactSection.astro and thanks.astro too. */}
  <p class="form-note">One reply from a person, within one business day, usually sooner. No sales sequence.</p>
</form>

<style>
  .form {
    background: var(--color-white); border: 1px solid var(--color-ink);
    border-radius: 0; padding: 30px;
    max-width: 100%;
  }
  .hp {
    position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden; clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* Sentence case at nav size, not uppercase micro. These labels are
     questions ("What does your business do?"), and a question set in 11px
     uppercase with .14em tracking reads as a system message rather than as
     something a person is asking. --color-body, not --color-faint: faint is
     3.66:1 on the card ground and fails AA for text at this size. */
  .form label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-nav); font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-mono); color: var(--color-body);
    display: block; margin-bottom: 7px;
  }
  .form input, .form textarea, .form select {
    width: 100%; padding: 11px 13px; border: 1px solid var(--color-ink);
    border-radius: 0; background: var(--color-paper);
    font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-ui);
    color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .form input:focus, .form textarea:focus, .form select:focus {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent-dark); outline-offset: -1px;
    border-color: var(--color-accent-dark);
  }
  .form textarea { resize: vertical; min-height: 82px; }
  /* 16px, not 17. --text-ui exists for this: 16 is the threshold at which iOS
     stops zooming the page when a field is focused, so this may not go lower.
     See the type scale in global.css. */

  .form .btn { width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; min-height: 50px; text-align: center; }
  .arw { margin-left: 9px; }

  /* Sits under the button, so it reads as a promise about what happens next
     rather than as a field hint. Sans at the .cta-note size. */
  .form-note {
    font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-small); line-height: var(--leading-body);
    color: var(--color-faint); margin-top: 16px; text-align: center;
  }
</style>
